# Scheduled work, run by supercronic in the worker container.
#
# Five fields: minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week. Times are read in
# the container's timezone, so the worker sets TZ=Europe/London -- without it
# these would run on UTC and drift by an hour for half the year, silently.
#
# Output goes to stdout and is collected by Docker's logging, which is why this
# is supercronic rather than cron: no MAILTO, no /var/log, nothing to go and read
# inside a container.
#
# A line whose command does not exist yet stays commented out until it does. A
# job that fails every time it runs is noise in the logs, and noise is how a real
# failure gets missed.

# Re-read the knowledge base repository and re-embed anything that changed.
#
# Hourly rather than daily, and it is worth being clear about why, because the
# obvious reading is that this runs a hundred times more often than it needs to.
#
# A run with nothing to do costs one request to the GitHub tree API and about a
# second: every file's blob SHA matches what is stored, so nothing is downloaded
# and nothing is embedded. Twenty-four of those a day is not a cost.
#
# What the frequency buys is a bound on staleness -- an article published at
# 09:05 is answerable by 10:00 rather than the following morning -- and, more
# importantly, this is the only thing that proves the pipeline still works. A
# webhook that stops firing is indistinguishable from a repository nobody has
# committed to. A scheduled run that stops succeeding says so in the logs.
#
# It also reconciles drift that never came from a commit: a changed embedding
# model, a half-finished run, rows removed by hand. Run `--dry-run` by hand when
# you want to see what it would do, and run it without the flag when you have
# just published something and do not want to wait for the hour.
0 * * * *    python -m portfolio_ai.ingestion

# The weekly digest: what visitors asked, what the assistant could not answer,
# which content gaps are worth writing. Monday morning, before the week starts.
# Lands with build step 7, once there is traffic worth a digest.
# 30 5 * * 1   python -m portfolio_ai.analytics digest

# Retention sweep. Chat messages and feedback older than CHAT_RETENTION_DAYS are
# deleted, and so are sessions nobody has come back to; content gaps, aggregates
# and promoted eval cases are kept, so the insight outlives the conversation it
# came from. Nightly, so nothing outlives the promise by more than a day. Run it
# with --dry-run by hand to see what it would delete.
0 3 * * *    python -m portfolio_ai.analytics purge
